				\begin{document}$$MCC=\frac{(TP\times TN)-(FP\times FN)}{\sqrt{(TP+FP)\times (TP+FN)\times (FP+TN)\times (TN+FN)}}$$\end{document}MCC=(TP×TN)−(FP×FN)(TP+FP)×(TP+FN)×(FP+TN)×(TN+FN)where from the confusion matrix, TP (true positive) and TN (true negative) are correctly predicted positive and negative samples, respectively. Similarly, FP (false positive) and FN (false negative) are incorrectly predicted positive and negative samples, respectively. Among these measures, MCC is the most stringent one; it takes into account both accuracy and error rates of the two classes. MCC close to 1.0 means that the classifier has high accuracy and a low misclassification rate.

